---
/**
 * Your own baseline, one writer at a time — and what SDNN is not. Rendered by
 * /science/sleep-heart/night-hrv; see scienceTopics.ts.
 *
 * The wearables table below is checked against each vendor's own pages
 * before every release of the app; a row that cannot be confirmed is removed
 * rather than left to age.
 */
import CitationCard from "../../components/CitationCard.astro";
import { NIGHT_HRV_SECTION } from "../../data/science";
import { topicHref } from "../../data/scienceTopics";
const rhr = topicHref("resting-heart-rate");
const hrr = topicHref("heart-rate-recovery");
const sleep = topicHref("sleep-rhythm");

const wearables: Array<[string, string, string, string]> = [
  ["Apple Watch", "SDNN", "Yes, as SDNN", "Samples of about 1 minute, several a night; Verve takes the median of the ones inside the sleep window."],
  ["Oura", "rMSSD", "No HRV, per third-party reports", "Sleep and resting rate reach Apple Health; the HRV stays in the Oura app."],
  ["WHOOP", "rMSSD", "No HRV, per third-party reports", "WHOOP's stated reason: writing rMSSD into a field defined as SDNN would mislead."],
  ["Garmin", "rMSSD", "No HRV, per third-party reports", "Garmin's own status reads rMSSD over the night; it is not written to Health."],
  ["HRV4Training, Elite HRV", "rMSSD-based", "Yes, into the SDNN field", "A chest-strap or camera reading, written by the app under Apple's one HRV type."],
];
---

<div class="mt-[18px] rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <p class="font-mono text-[9px] uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-text-tertiary">
    How a night is read
  </p>
  <div class="mt-3 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {[
      ["The reading", "the median of the samples inside the night's sleep window, ms"],
      ["The usual", "the median of your last 30 nights, from the same writer"],
      ["The band", "the middle 50% of those 30 nights"],
      ["The card", "the writer and its method: Apple Watch · SDNN"],
      ["What it is not", "a population figure, a readiness score, a verdict"],
    ].map(([k, v]) => (
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-0.5 rounded-xl border border-white/8 bg-white/[0.03] px-3 py-2.5">
        <span class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-text-tertiary">{k}</span>
        <span class="text-[12.5px] text-text-secondary">{v}</span>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</div>

<div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
  <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    Heart rate variability is the beat-to-beat unevenness of a resting heart.
    A heart that speeds a little on each breath in and slows on each breath
    out is being steered by the vagus nerve; the more freely it is steered,
    the larger the variation. That is what the number measures, and it is all
    it measures. It is not a fitness score, and it is not a readiness score,
    whatever the dashboard beside it says.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Why night only.</strong>
    A watch samples HRV through the day, and the daytime samples are
    contaminated: standing up, talking, a flight of stairs and a coffee all
    change the interval between beats for reasons that have nothing to do
    with the nervous system at rest. The night is the one stretch of the
    day when the body is still, the breathing is regular and the
    sympathetic drive is low, so the night is the only window Verve reads.
    The reading for a night is the median of the samples that fall inside
    that night&rsquo;s sleep window, the same window the
    <a href={sleep}>sleep timing</a> page describes. A median, not a mean, so
    one sample taken during a turn in bed does not drag the night with it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Your own baseline, nothing against a population.</strong>
    The usual is the median of your own last 30 nights with a reading, and
    the band is the middle 50% of those nights: the quarter above and the
    quarter below are left outside it, so a night inside the band is a night
    like most of yours. There is no population figure to compare against,
    and Verve does not print one. HRV spans roughly a fivefold range between
    healthy people of the same age, and it falls with age in everyone, so a
    &ldquo;normal&rdquo; of 20 to 100&nbsp;ms says nothing about whether
    your 34 is good. Whether your 34 is your usual 34 or a fall from your
    usual 48 is the only question the number can answer, and it is the same
    rule <a href={rhr}>resting heart rate</a> is read by.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">SDNN and rMSSD, plainly.</strong>
    Two ways to turn a list of beat intervals into one number.
    <em>SDNN</em> is the standard deviation of all the intervals in the
    window: how widely the beats are spread around their own average. It
    takes in every rhythm present, fast and slow, and because the slow
    rhythms need time to show, its value depends on how long the window is;
    a 1-minute SDNN and a 24-hour SDNN are different quantities with one
    name. <em>rMSSD</em> is the root mean square of the differences between
    each beat and the next: how much the interval jumps from one beat to the
    one after. Only a fast rhythm can move a beat against its neighbour, and
    the fast rhythm at rest is breathing, steered by the vagus nerve, so
    rMSSD is close to a pure read of vagal tone and barely cares how long the
    window is. Its cost is that it needs clean beats: a single missed or
    doubled beat is a large jump, and the squaring makes it larger.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Which is better.</strong>
    At night, at rest, with the sympathetic drive low, the two track each
    other closely, because both are then dominated by the same thing: the
    breathing-driven vagal variation. rMSSD is the better short-window index
    of vagal tone and the research favourite for athlete monitoring; the
    papers on training load are written in it. SDNN over Apple&rsquo;s
    windows of about 1 minute is noisier sample by sample, but a night&rsquo;s
    median of those samples is a sound within-person trend. For a personal
    baseline neither is &ldquo;better&rdquo;, provided the two are never
    mixed on one line: an rMSSD of 60 and an SDNN of 60 are not the same
    night, and a chart that holds both is a chart of the switch between
    them.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/20">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[620px] text-[12.5px]">
      <thead>
        <tr class="text-left font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-text-tertiary">
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Wearable</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Computes</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Writes to Apple Health</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Note</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/[0.06]">
        {wearables.map(([w, m, writes, note]) => (
          <tr class="align-top">
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">{w}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-mono text-text-secondary">{m}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">{writes}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">{note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    Apple Health has one HRV type, and its definition is SDNN. Apple Watch
    writes SDNN into it. Oura, WHOOP and Garmin compute rMSSD and, per
    third-party reports, do not write HRV to Apple Health at all. Some
    chest-strap and camera apps write rMSSD-based values into the SDNN
    field, which is why Verve reads the writer&rsquo;s name as well as the
    number. This table is checked against the vendors&rsquo; pages before
    each release.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Verve&rsquo;s rules.</strong>
    One writer per night: a night with samples from two sources is read
    from one of them, never averaged across both. The writer and its method
    are printed on the card, &ldquo;Apple Watch · SDNN&rdquo;, or
    &ldquo;HRV4Training · rMSSD&rdquo;, or &ldquo;method undeclared&rdquo;
    where the writer does not say. The baseline is kept per writer, so a new
    writer starts a new band from its own first night rather than being
    judged against the old writer&rsquo;s. Two methods never share a line.
    And there is no readiness score: a number that folds HRV, sleep and
    resting rate into one figure with a cheerful word under it has no study
    behind the fold.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">When the Guide says anything at all.</strong>
    A single low night is a night: a late meal, a drink, heat, a hard
    session and the first day of a cold all do this, and the number cannot
    tell them apart. The Guide names a pattern only when night HRV,
    <a href={rhr}>resting heart rate</a> and
    <a href={hrr}>one-minute recovery</a> all leave their own bands in the
    same direction, HRV low, resting rate high, recovery small, for 3 nights.
    Three readings agreeing for three nights is a pattern worth a sentence.
    One reading on one night is not, and the Guide stays quiet.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
  {NIGHT_HRV_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
</div>
